===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|stān}}, {{m|gmh|stēn}}, from {{inh|de|goh|stān}}, {{m|goh|stēn}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*stān}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*stāną}}. The ''-h-'' was introduced into the spelling by analogy with {{m|de|sehen}}, in which it had become mute but was retained in spelling. 

Past forms are inherited from {{inh|de|goh|stuond}}, {{m|goh|stuonden}}, etc. These are themselves [[suppletive]] of {{w|West Germanic languages|Proto-West Germanic}} {{m|gmw-pro|*standan}}'s past forms in ''*stōnd-'', replacing former forms inherited from {{m|gmw-pro|*stān}} in ''*stōþ-''. Compare english [[stood]]. Their a-vocalism is due to analogy with other strong verbs such as {{m|de|binden}} (''band'') and {{m|de|finden}} (''fand''), dating back to {{w|Middle High German}}. See also [[stand]].

